{"log": {"at": "2026-08-16T18:05:00-05:00", "msg": "Two ranges were in direct CONFLICT and it took the presets to show it: pad-share as a raw share of width wants 3.7% of a 3440 band, which is 127px, which is 7em, which frame-gap calls badly over-padded. Both bands came from the same 36 pages and both are right — about different boxes. polish.js already writes the resolution (min(3.5%, 3.5em), proportional until a box gets wide then flat), so pad-share now measures against that expectation: a 300px card with 11px and a 3440 band with 56px both read 1.0. frame-gaps ceiling also had to clear 3.5, because that is where the sites own formula lands a band at 3440 — a ceiling of 3.2 made the house pattern unrepresentable."}}
{"log": {"at": "2026-08-16T18:07:00-05:00", "msg": "Biggest single generator win of the session: --pad INHERITS, so the band inset the generator put on a wide top-level track was also the inset on the 300px card inside it — three ems on a card the rulebook expects ten pixels on. Every leaf now reclaims a cards pad. Measured on 14 seeds at chaos 0: mean worst 56 to 66, median 54 to 68, best 65 to 75."}}
{"log": {"at": "2026-08-16T18:25:00-05:00", "msg": "A dial whose sign flips under measurement is measuring the wrong thing. Making chaos draw depth uniformly over 0..max produced FLATTER layouts at max 2 — and this rulebook rewards simplicity, so chaos measured as an improvement (mean 61 to 70 over 8 seeds). Reverted: DEPTHS now spans 0-10 instead of stopping at 5 (its keys are the vocabulary draw.js blends toward uniform, so the dials right half was unreachable at any chaos) and level() is a plain weighted pick again. Final verification sweep dispatched at n=120 to settle the sign."}}
{"action":{"at":"2026-08-16T15:21:44-05:00","did":"edit","files":[".claude/skills/layout-design/SKILL.md"]}}
{"log": {"at": "2026-08-16T18:55:00-05:00", "msg": "Run 3, n=120, after the four fixes. Chaos sign SETTLED: mean worst 73.8 / 72.6 / 65.9 at chaos 0 / 0.25 / 1.0, monotonic, -10.7%. The padding fix was worth +8.3 points of mean fitness on the same scorer (65.5 to 73.8) — the cleanest before/after in the exercise. New ceiling 86, and it is 86 at 390, 86 at 1280 AND 86 at 3440: every earlier ceiling had one width dragging it down, and a flat curve is what the prime objective actually asks for. The shape table went flat (lift 0.98-1.12, leader n=6, under the believability floor) so SHAPES was LEFT ALONE — fitting a table that flat is fitting noise, and a loop has to be able to decline to run."}}
{"log": {"at": "2026-08-16T18:57:00-05:00", "msg": "THE LESSON OF THE SESSION: a broken range discriminates beautifully. width-used, lanes and frame-gap each looked like a strong discriminator and each collapsed to a ~0.0 top-vs-bottom gap the moment its own defect was fixed. Three times is not a coincidence — a defective range separates layouts BY THE DEFECT. width-used was sorting them by how much text they happened to contain; frame-gap by how many undecorated swatches they drew. Both signals were real and reproducible; neither was about the layout. A ranges apparent power is not evidence that it is right, and a suspiciously clean ranking is the first thing to distrust."}}
{"assign": {"step": 8, "landed_at": "2026-08-16T19:15:00-05:00", "outcome": "**The layout generator now searches instead of sampling, and it got measurably stronger doing it.** New: [/framework/ext/LayoutTool/taste/](/framework/ext/LayoutTool/taste/) — a third tier that rates what is GOOD (eleven weighted ideal ranges) where analyze() only says what is broken; [/framework/styles/layouts/space/hunt/](/framework/styles/layouts/space/hunt/) — the search, and where the model gets better; [/framework/dev/Claim/](/framework/dev/Claim/) — the viewport ring an agent draws around a tab it is driving, with a claim-tab MCP skill. Rebuilt: the generator draws from a grammar (shapes, roles, what-is-best-inside-what) instead of a flat pick, with a chaos dial that is a measured distance from that model and tones taken from the sites own colour tokens. Unified: gen(seed, opts) takes an options object where it took a depth, so every ext/Panel reroll button inherits the same model and chaos — with zero edits to ext/Panel. MEASURED, n=120 x 3 widths: chaos hurts monotonically (-10.7%), the padding-cascade fix was worth +8.3 points of mean fitness, and the best layout found scores 86 at 390, 86 at 1280 and 86 at 3440 — every earlier ceiling had one width dragging it down. The rulebooks own bands were refit twice, six on calibration against 36 site pages and four more on use. The lesson worth keeping: a BROKEN range discriminates beautifully, because it is measuring its own defect — three of eleven went quiet the moment their bug was fixed.", "window": {"before": 0.17, "after": 0.24}, "tokens": 82494022, "usage": {"input": 6326, "cache_write": 417636, "cache_read": 81849230, "output": 220830, "calls": 280}, "links": [{"url": "/framework/ext/LayoutTool/taste/", "label": "LayoutTool taste — the eleven ideal ranges, live"}, {"url": "/framework/styles/layouts/space/", "label": "Space — the lab, with the chaos dial and a rating per screen"}, {"url": "/framework/styles/layouts/space/hunt/", "label": "Hunt — the search, and the weights it would write"}, {"url": "/framework/styles/layouts/space/compose/", "label": "Compose — the same roll as real panels, same dials"}, {"url": "/framework/dev/Claim/", "label": "dev/Claim — the tab claim ring"}]}}
